title: Schema
kind: overview
aliases: schema.json mf_schema
summary: Declare types of metarecords and the fields they may carry — value type and how many rows — and the daemon refuses writes that break them.

A repository may have a ''user schema'': a JSON file declaring ''types'' of
metarecords and ''constraints'' on their fields — which value type a field holds,
how many rows of it a metarecord may have. Constraints can also apply to every
metarecord, typically to fix the type of a field name across the repository.

A metarecord says its type with the reserved field `mf_schema`. The daemon checks
each ''write'' against the schema and refuses a violating one with `400`; existing
data is never rewritten, and a check lists what already violates it. The store
holds no constraint: the schema is a file you edit.

Independently of any schema, a field name holds one value type across the
repository ([[One value type per field name]]): a schema `type`
declares that type explicitly — before any write, and for documentation — and
one that contradicts the type already established is a schema error.

